message:
OK so the `if(c == 0) break;` was added in commit bf8ee636. Now let me understand the current code. The loop is:

```c
for (ix = 0; ix < (N_WORD_BYTES-1);) {
    c = p_start[ix];
    word_copy[ix++] = c;
    if (c == 0)
        break;
}
word_copy[ix] = 0;
```

With `N_WORD_BYTES = 160`, the loop runs while `ix < 159`. So `ix` can go up to 158 inside the loop, and then `word_copy[159] = 0` is valid (index 159 is the last element of a 160-element array).

Wait, but let me re-examine. If `ix` reaches 158 inside the loop, it copies `p_start[158]` to `word_copy[158]`, increments `ix` to 159, and then checks `if (c == 0) break;`. If `c != 0`, the loop condition `ix < 159` fails, so the loop exits. Then `word_copy[159] = 0` is valid.

So the loop itself seems safe. But wait - what if `N_WORD_BYTES` is different in the actual vulnerable version? Let me check what `N_WORD_BYTES` is defined as:

<｜｜DSML｜｜>
<parameter=command>cd /workspace/repo-vul/src-vul/espeak-ng && grep -rn "N_WORD_BYTES" src/libespeak-ng/ | head -20</｜｜DSML｜｜>
</｜｜DSML｜｜>

